+(**
+# Which `open` do I need?
+
+Most of F#+ arrives with a single `open FSharpPlus`, but not all of it, and the parts that need their
+own `open` are not obvious from the namespace layout. Optics in particular live in a module *under*
+`FSharpPlus`, which makes it look as though opening the parent namespace should be enough. It is not.
+
+## What `open FSharpPlus` gives you
+
+Four modules in the library are marked `[<AutoOpen>]`, so opening the `FSharpPlus` namespace brings
+their contents directly into scope:
+
+| Module | What it contains |
+|--------|------------------|
+| `Operators`       | the generic functions and operators - `map`, `bind`, `traverse`, `sequence`, `result`, and the operator set |
+| `GenericBuilders` | the computation expressions - `monad`, `applicative`, `zapp` and friends |
+| `Parsing`         | `parse`, `tryParse`, `parseArray`, `tryParseArray` and the `Parsed` active pattern |
+| `Memoization`     | `memoizeN` |
+
+The extension modules - `String`, `Option`, `Result`, `Seq`, `List`, `Map`, `Array`, `Task`, `Async`
+and the rest - are declared in the `FSharpPlus` namespace as `[<RequireQualifiedAccess>]` modules, so
+after `open FSharpPlus` they are reachable qualified, as `String.toLower`.
+
+## What needs its own `open`
+
+Everything else, including two things people reach for constantly:
+
+| Module or namespace | Why it is separate |
+|---------------------|--------------------|
+| `FSharpPlus.Data`             | a namespace of its own, holding the types |
+| `FSharpPlus.Lens`             | a plain module under `FSharpPlus`, **not** auto-opened |
+| `FSharpPlus.Math.Generic`     | a plain module in a separate namespace |
+| `FSharpPlus.Math.Applicative` | lifts arithmetic into applicatives |
+| `FSharpPlus.Control`          | the trait types, needed only when writing your own instances |
+
+## Reference
+
+| What you want | What to open |
+|---------------|--------------|
+| `map`, `bind`, `join`, `traverse`, `sequence`, `result`, `foldMap`, `length` | `open FSharpPlus` |
+| operators such as `<!>`, `<*>`, `>>=`, `>=>`, `++`, `<`&#124;`>`, `<.>` | `open FSharpPlus` |
+| `monad`, `monad'`, `monad.plus`, `applicative`, `zapp` | `open FSharpPlus` |
+| `tryParse`, `parse`, `memoizeN` | `open FSharpPlus` |
+| `String.toLower`, `Option.*`, `Result.*`, `Seq.*` and the other extension modules | `open FSharpPlus` |
+| `Reader`, `Writer`, `State`, `Cont`, `Validation`, `NonEmptyList`, `DList`, `ZipList`, `Identity` | `open FSharpPlus.Data` |
+| `Dual`, `Mult`, `First`, `Last`, `All`, `Any` | `open FSharpPlus.Data` |
+| `OptionT`, `ResultT`, `ReaderT`, `WriterT`, `StateT`, `SeqT`, `ListT`, `ContT` | `open FSharpPlus.Data` |
+| `view`, `preview`, `setl`, `over`, `_1`, `_2`, `_Some`, `_Ok`, `toListOf` | `open FSharpPlus.Lens` |
+| the optics operators `^.`, `^?`, `^..`, `.->`, `%->`, `<&>` | `open FSharpPlus.Lens` |
+| generic numeric operations over any numeric type | `open FSharpPlus.Math.Generic` |
+| arithmetic lifted into applicatives | `open FSharpPlus.Math.Applicative` |
+| writing your own instances - `Map.Invoke`, `Bind.Invoke` and similar | `open FSharpPlus.Control` |
+
+## Optics usually need two or three opens
+
+`open FSharpPlus.Lens` brings in the optics themselves, but any surrounding generic function still
+comes from `open FSharpPlus`, and monoid wrappers come from `open FSharpPlus.Data`. A lens example
+that also folds or traverses therefore needs all three:
+*)
+
+open FSharpPlus
+open FSharpPlus.Lens
+
+let firstOf : int = (1, "a") ^. _1
+
+// val firstOf : int = 1
+
+let updated : int * string = (1, "a") |> (_1 .-> 99)
+
+// val updated : int * string = (99, "a")
+
+(**
+By contrast `tryParse` comes from the auto-opened `Parsing` module, so `open FSharpPlus` alone is
+enough:
+*)
+
+let parsedInt : int option = tryParse "42"
+
+// val parsedInt : int option = Some 42
+
+let failedParse : int option = tryParse "nope"
+
+// val failedParse : int option = None
+
+(**
+## Two things worth knowing
+
+**The extension modules shadow the ones in FSharp.Core.** `String`, `Option`, `Result`, `List`, `Seq`,
+`Map` and `Array` all exist in the `FSharpPlus` namespace, so after `open FSharpPlus` a call such as
+`Option.map` may resolve to the F#+ one. That is normally what you want and is source compatible, but
+it does mean the order of your `open` declarations can change which function you get.
+
+**Prefer explicit opens in each example you write.** Anyone copying a snippet takes the snippet, not
+the whole file, so a snippet that silently depends on an `open` several hundred lines earlier will not
+compile where it lands.
+*)
